In this lesson we'll look at radians, a really neat way to measure angles using a unit circle, which is just a circle with a radius of one. The big idea is beautifully simple: instead of chopping a circle into 360 equal pieces like degrees do, you wrap a number line around the circle, and the angle in radians is exactly the arc length you sweep out from the positive x-axis. So one full trip around the circle is two pi radians, which is the same as 360 degrees. Half a turn is pi radians, or 180 degrees, and one radian is roughly 57.3 degrees. Now here's the payoff. When you rotate an angle theta anticlockwise from the starting point on the positive x-axis, you land on a point whose coordinates are cosine of theta and sine of theta. That's how the unit circle gives you sine and cosine for free! Quick trick to remember the signs in each quadrant: in quadrant one both are positive, in quadrant two sine is positive but cosine is negative, in quadrant three both are negative, and in quadrant four cosine is positive but sine is negative. Now let's see it in action.